course: dictionary
lesson: 6503
title: The word née in family names
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to formal writing. You meet them in family trees, history books and old newspapers. Née is one of them. In «Mrs Jane Smith, née Brown», the word née tells you that Jane's surname before she married was Brown, and Smith is her married name.
explain: The word comes from French, where «née» means «born». So the name after née is the name a woman was born with. Look for the clue: née sits between a woman's married name and her name from birth.
explain: Do not confuse it with nǐ, a Mandarin word that sounds similar. «nǐ jiào shénme míngzi?» means «what's your name?», and nǐ means «you». Same sound, different word. The context tells them apart.
fact: Née means born, and it is used before a woman's family name from birth.
fact: «Mrs Jane Smith, née Brown» means Jane was born Jane Brown and married Mr Smith.
fact: Nee is often written without the accent, but the meaning stays the same.
fact: A related form, né, is sometimes used before a man's earlier name.
fact: Sound-alike words: «nǐ» (Mandarin) means you, as in «nǐ xǐhuan shénme yánsè?», which means «what colour do you like?»
exercise: In «Mrs Jane Smith, née Brown», what does née tell you? | Her job was a teacher | Her married name is Brown | She lives in Brown Street | *Her surname before she married was Brown
exercise: Née comes from which language, where it means «born»? | Latin | German | *French | Spanish
exercise: In «nǐ xǐhuan shénme yánsè?», what does nǐ mean? | Born | *You | Colour | Blue
test: «Mrs Ruth Patel, née Shah.» Which name is her married name? | Shah | Mrs | Ruth | *Patel
test: Why might a family tree use née? | *To show a woman's surname from birth | To show where she lives now | To show her favourite colour | Because the writer likes French
exam: In «Mrs Ann Cole, née Ward», what does née mean? | Cole is her birth name | *Ann's surname before marriage was Ward | Ward is a street name | Ann was born in Ward
